U-BLOX NEO M8

I2C Helper (ublox_i2c_read_s32): This function handles the low-level I2C communication. It uses i2c_smbus_read_i2c_block_data to read 4 bytes starting from the given register address (either 0x0A or 0xA4). It assumes the common standard of Big Endian format for multi-byte sensor data and converts it to the CPU's native endianness.

    Channel Specification (ublox_channels):

        It defines two channels: Latitude and Longitude.

        It uses the appropriate IIO types (IIO_POSITIONID) and modifiers (IIO_MOD_LAT/IIO_MOD_LON).

        The address field in iio_chan_spec is used to store your custom register addresses (0x0A, 0xA4).

        The scan_type is set to s32 (signed 32-bit) with IIO_BE (Big Endian) for buffer processing.

    Direct Read (ublox_read_raw): This implements the standard user-space read hook (e.g., when a user runs cat /sys/.../in_position_lat_raw). It calls the I2C helper to fetch the current coordinate instantly.

    Probe Function (ublox_probe):

        It allocates and initializes the IIO device structure.

        It retrieves the interrupt number (irq = client->irq) provided by the DTS.

        It registers a threaded IRQ handler (ublox_irq_handler) which simply signals the IIO core that the trigger has fired.

        It calls iio_triggered_buffer_setup to enable the buffered path, linking the trigger to the data processing function (ublox_trigger_handler).

    Trigger Handler (ublox_trigger_handler): This is the heart of the triggered path. When the IRQ fires, this function executes:

        It iterates over all enabled channels (indio_dev->active_scan_mask).

        For each channel, it calls the I2C helper to read the 4-byte coordinate.

        It stores the data in the private data->buffer.

        Finally, it pushes the entire buffer content, along with a high-resolution kernel timestamp, to user-space using iio_push_to_buffers_with_timestamp.

Example Device Tree Node (DTS)

To use this driver, your system's device tree would need an entry like this (assuming your hardware uses I2C bus 1 and the device is at address 0x42, with the data-ready signal tied to GPIO pin 10):

&i2c1 {
    status = "okay";
    
    ublox_gps: gps@42 {
        compatible = "vendor,ublox-gps-receiver"; // Matches the driver's compatible string
        reg = <0x42>; // I2C address
        interrupt-parent = <&gpio>;
        interrupts = <10 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW>; // Use GPIO 10, triggered on low level
    };
};
